#{[17592186120323 "A vector $\\mathbf b$ lies in $\\operatorname{Null}(A)$ exactly when $A\\mathbf b=\\mathbf0$. Compute each product:\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf b_1=\\begin{bmatrix}16\\\\-13\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad A\\mathbf b_2=\\begin{bmatrix}14\\\\0\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad A\\mathbf b_3=\\begin{bmatrix}-10\\\\2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNone of these is the zero vector, so none of the three vectors lies in $\\operatorname{Null}(A)$."] [17592186120334 "A vector $\\mathbf b$ lies in $\\operatorname{Null}(A)$ exactly when $A\\mathbf b=\\mathbf0$. Compute each product:\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf b_1=\\begin{bmatrix}0\\\\0\\\\0\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad A\\mathbf b_2=\\begin{bmatrix}0\\\\0\\\\0\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad A\\mathbf b_3=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\\\8\\\\2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf b_1$ and $\\mathbf b_2$ only lie in $\\operatorname{Null}(A)$."] [17592186120376 "Starting from $\\mathbf u\\cdot\\mathbf v=11$, compute the dot product and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac4{x^2}x^4+4\\cdot0+8x+7(-7)&=11\\\\4x^2+8x-60&=0\\\\x^2+2x-15&=0\\\\(x+5)(x-3)&=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vector requires $x\\ne0$, and both roots satisfy this restriction. Since $x<0$, the required root is $x=-5$."] [17592186125167 "Since $\\det(A)\\ne0$, the matrix $A$ is invertible. Thus statement I is false.\n\nThe homogeneous equation $A\\mathbf x=\\mathbf0$ has only the trivial solution, so $\\operatorname{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf0\\}$. The empty set is linearly independent and its span is $\\{\\mathbf0\\}$, so it is a basis of this null space. Thus statement II is true.\n\nAn invertible $n\\times n$ matrix has a pivot in every column, so statement III is true. Therefore, the correct answer is II and III only."] [17592186125168 "If $A$ is an $7\\times 7$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{7} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A)) &=7=\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\ \\operatorname{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A)) &=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $\\det(A)=0$* $\\,\\times$ \n\nSince the matrix $A$ is invertible, it must be  $\\det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then  $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7}.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7}.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $A$ has $5$ pivot columns.* $\\,\\times$ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=7,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $7$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7}$ only."] [17592186134221 "To extend the orthonormal set, find a unit vector $\\mathbf x$ orthogonal to both given vectors. The equations are\n\n$$\n\\frac{x_1-2x_3}{\\sqrt5}=0,\\qquad \\frac{2x_1+2x_2+x_3}{3}=0.\n$$\n\nHence $x_1=2x_3$ and $x_2=-\\frac52x_3$, so $\\mathbf x=t\\begin{bmatrix}4\\\\-5\\\\2\\end{bmatrix}$. Its norm is $3\\sqrt5|t|$, so the unit-vector condition gives $|t|=\\frac1{3\\sqrt5}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n|x_1|+|x_2|+|x_3|=\\frac{4+5+2}{3\\sqrt5}=\\frac{11}{3\\sqrt5}.\n$$"] [17592186134222 "A set is orthogonal when every pair of distinct vectors has dot product zero. Denoting the vectors in each displayed set by subscripts, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u_1\\cdot u_2&=1+6-1-6=0,&u_1\\cdot u_3&=2-2+2-2=0,&u_2\\cdot u_3&=2-3-2+3=0,\\\\v_1\\cdot v_2&=2-4+2=0,&v_1\\cdot v_3&=-12+0+12=0,&v_2\\cdot v_3&=-6+0+6=0,\\\\w_1\\cdot w_2&=-8+2=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $U$ and $V$ are orthogonal, while $W$ is not. The correct answer is $U$ and $V$ only."] [17592186152581 "To find a basis of the column space, reduce $A$ and select the corresponding pivot columns of the original matrix. Its reduced row echelon form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1&0&\\frac32\\\\0&1&\\frac12\\\\0&0&0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are columns 1 and 2. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2\\\\1\\\\3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6\\\\-1\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\operatorname{Col}(A)$. The third column equals $\\frac32$ times the first plus $\\frac12$ times the second."] [17592186152583 "Swap rows 1 and 3, divide row 2 by 3, and subtract twice row 2 from row 1. This gives the reduced row echelon form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1&0&0\\\\0&0&1\\\\0&0&0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nColumns 1 and 3 are pivot columns. Select those columns from the original matrix:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0\\\\0\\\\1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0\\\\3\\\\2\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThese independent vectors span the column space. A basis cannot include the zero second column."] [17592186152721 "The first two columns are $\\mathbf a_1=(1,0,1)^T$ and $\\mathbf a_2=(0,5,0)^T$. Every linear combination has equal first and third components. However, $\\mathbf a_4=(1,5,2)^T$ does not have equal first and third components, so it is outside their span.\n\nThus $\\{\\mathbf a_1,\\mathbf a_2\\}$ does not span $\\operatorname{Col}(A)$ and is not a basis. The conditional request for $x_1x_2$ does not apply."] [17592186184065 "Using the axioms of a vector space, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w})+(-\\mathbf{w}) & =\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }-\\mathbf{w}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+(\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w})) & =\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom }{\\color{blue}\\text{A2}}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{0} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A4}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom }{\\color{blue}\\text{A3}}\\hspace{0.222em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247208 "If $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $A\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\mathbf{b} & =\\mathbf{0} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}28 & -7 & 14 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ k \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-7k+14 \\\\ k-2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-7k+14 &=0 \\\\ k-2 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=2.$"] [17592186247215 "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(G),$ we reduce the matrix $G$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${\\mathbf{g}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{g}}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(G)$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{g}}_{1},{\\mathbf{g}}_{3}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"] [17592186247216 "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(F),$ we reduce the matrix $F$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 12 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${\\mathbf{f}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{f}}_{1},{\\mathbf{f}}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"] [17592186247217 "We need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe second equation tells us that ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+3(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that $\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the only solution to this system.\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$"] [17592186247221 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 24 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{16}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{d}}_{1},{\\mathbf{d}}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${\\mathbf{d}}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{d}}_{2}}=\\underbrace{(-6)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{d}}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(0)}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{d}}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=-6\\cdot 0=0.$"] [17592186247222 "First, we reduce the matrix $T$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 30\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{t}}_{1},{\\mathbf{t}}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${\\mathbf{t}}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{t}}_{3}}=\\underbrace{(2)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{t}}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(3)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{t}}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 3=6.$"] [17592186247223 "First, we reduce the matrix $T$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{t}}_{1},{\\mathbf{t}}_{2}\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\left\\{{\\mathbf{t}}_{1},{\\mathbf{t}}_{3}\\right\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(T).$"] [17592186247225 "First, we reduce the matrix $T$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{12}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{t}}_{1},{\\mathbf{t}}_{3}\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\left\\{{\\mathbf{t}}_{1},{\\mathbf{t}}_{2}\\right\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(T).$"] [17592186247227 "First, we reduce the matrix $C$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -12 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -12 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(C)$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{c}}_{1},{\\mathbf{c}}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${\\mathbf{c}}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{c}}_{4}}=\\underbrace{(4)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{c}}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(3)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{c}}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=4\\cdot 3=12.$"] [17592186247228 "First, we reduce the matrix $R$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 12 & -6 & 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -6 & 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-12){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -6 & -18\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -6 & -18\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(R)$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{r}}_{1},{\\mathbf{r}}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{r}}_{1},{\\mathbf{r}}_{2},{\\mathbf{r}}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$"] [17592186247232 "First, we reduce the matrix $R$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(R)$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{r}}_{1},{\\mathbf{r}}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{r}}_{1},{\\mathbf{r}}_{2},{\\mathbf{r}}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$"] [17592186247233 "First, we reduce the matrix $S$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(S)$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{s}}_{1},{\\mathbf{s}}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{s}}_{1},{\\mathbf{s}}_{2},{\\mathbf{s}}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(S).$"] [17592186272104 "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{\\mathbf{u}}_{1},{\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\},$ \n$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{\\mathbf{w}}_{1},{\\mathbf{w}}_{2},{\\mathbf{w}}_{3}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2}=0.$\n\n- $$\n{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2}=0,\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{3}=0,\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{w}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{3}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=10-9-1=0 & {\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=4-4+4=4\\ne 0 & {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=10-9-1=0 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ {\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=0+3-3=0 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ {\\mathbf{w}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=0-3+3=0 & {\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."] [17592186272106 "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{\\mathbf{u}}_{1},{\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\},$ \n$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{\\mathbf{w}}_{1},{\\mathbf{w}}_{2}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $$\n{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}=0\\hspace{1em}\\text{and}\\hspace{1em}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}=0\\hspace{1em}\\text{and}\\hspace{1em}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2}=0\\hspace{1em}\\text{and}\\hspace{1em}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {\\mathbf{w}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & {\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}+0-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}=0 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ {\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{4}{9}=1 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ {\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}=1 & {\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice tha