\\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & {\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=4+9=13\\ne 1 & {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{4}{29}-\\frac{25}{29}=-1\\ne 0 & {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ only\"."] [17592186272108 "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${\\mathbf{u}}_{1},\\hspace{0.167em}{\\mathbf{u}}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot \\mathbf{x} &=0 \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\cdot \\mathbf{x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3(\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3})+2{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{x}_{3}\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=4,$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-5}{1}=-5.\n$$"] [17592186272109 "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${\\mathbf{u}}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $\\mathbf{x}$ such that $‖\\mathbf{x}‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot \\mathbf{x}=0\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{x}_{2}\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $\\mathbf{x}.$ Notice that $‖\\mathbf{x}‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{10}.$ So, we normalize $\\mathbf{x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathbf{x}}{‖\\mathbf{x}‖}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle |{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}|=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$"] [17592186317166 "To compute the dot product, we add up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}+{p}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}+{p}_{3}\\cdot {q}_{3}+{p}_{4}\\cdot {q}_{4}+{p}_{5}\\cdot {q}_{5} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-10-4-4-9-10 \\\\  & =-37\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317169 "Starting from the equation $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=12,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $x$ and then solve for $x$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v} & =12 \\\\ 2{x}^{4}\\cdot 2{x}^{-2}+4\\cdot (-8x)+12\\cdot 4+6\\cdot 2 & =12 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-32x+48+12 & =12 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-32x+48 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe get two solutions, ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=6.$ Since we need $x<6$, our final answer is $x=2$ only."] [17592186317226 "We add the vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}+\\mathbf{s} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4+1 \\\\ 2+5 \\\\ -3+2 \\\\ 1-8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317387 "If $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $A\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\mathbf{b} & =\\mathbf{0} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}k+9 \\\\ 2k+18\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}k+9 &=0 \\\\ 2k+18 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=-9.$"] [17592186317441 "A vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A)$ if $A\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $\\mathbf{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{\\mathbf{b}}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 90\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\mathbf{0}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times } \\\\ A{\\mathbf{b}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-59 \\\\ 118 \\\\ -33\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\mathbf{0}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times } \\\\ A{\\mathbf{b}}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 180\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\mathbf{0}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, none of the vector lies in $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [17592186317454 "We need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe second equation tells us that ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that $\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the only solution to this system.\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$"] [17592186318978 "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(G),$ we reduce the matrix $G$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 2 & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}^{′} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, only the $1$st column is a pivot column, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st column from the original matrix is ${\\mathbf{g}}_{1}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(G)$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{g}}_{1}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"] [17592186318982 "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(F),$ we reduce the matrix $F$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 19\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$rd columns from the original matrix are ${\\mathbf{f}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{f}}_{1},{\\mathbf{f}}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"] [17592186319342 "If $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $A\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\mathbf{b} & =\\mathbf{0} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2k-8 \\\\ k+4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2k-8 &=0 \\\\ k+4 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=-4.$"] [17592186323706 "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w})+(-\\mathbf{w}) & =\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }(-\\mathbf{w})\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+(\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w})) & =\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A2}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{0} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom }{\\color{blue}\\text{A4}\\hspace{0.222em}]} \\\\ \\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom }{\\color{blue}\\text{A3}\\hspace{0.222em}]}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326761 "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{\\mathbf{u}}_{1},{\\mathbf{u}}_{2},{\\mathbf{u}}_{3}\\},$\n$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{\\mathbf{w}}_{1},{\\mathbf{w}}_{2},{\\mathbf{w}}_{3}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $$\n{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}=0,\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{3}=0,\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{3}=0.\n$$\n\n- ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2}=0.$\n\n- $$\n{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2}=0,\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{3}=0,\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{w}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{3}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=6-16+10=0 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0+0+0=0 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0+0+0=0 & {\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=-8+10=2\\ne 0 & {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=15-12-1=2\\ne 0 & {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ only\"."] [17592186326826 "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${\\mathbf{u}}_{1},\\hspace{0.167em}{\\mathbf{u}}_{2},\\hspace{0.167em}{\\mathbf{u}}_{3}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{4}$, we need to find a fourth vector $\\mathbf{x}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot \\mathbf{x} &=0 \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\cdot \\mathbf{x} &=0 \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{3}\\cdot \\mathbf{x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}-{x}_{4} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}+4{x}_{4} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 & 4 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are three pivot columns ($1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{4}$ is a free variable. From the third and first equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4}$, respectively. Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2}+2(-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4})-{x}_{4}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{x}_{4}\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{4}=5$, we obtain the vector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}=-\\frac{1}{5}.$"] [17592186326836 "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${\\mathbf{u}}_{1},\\hspace{0.167em}{\\mathbf{u}}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot \\mathbf{x} &=0 \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\cdot \\mathbf{x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}6 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -6 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}6 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -\\frac{37}{6} & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{37}{6}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{37}{6}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{x}_{3}\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=6$, we obtain the vector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -37 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -37 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{1}{37}.$"] [17592186342108 "Multiplying each component of $\\mathbf{v}$ by $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\mathbf{v} & =3\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 0 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 5 \\\\ 3\\cdot (-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186342109 "Multiplying each component of $\\mathbf{w}$ by $5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\mathbf{w} & =5\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5\\cdot 1 \\\\ 5\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 5\\cdot 3 \\\\ 5\\cdot (-4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 15 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186342112 "First, multiplying each component of $\\mathbf{a}$ by $\\frac{1}{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the dot product, we add up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a})\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =2\\cdot 5+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10+3+1+0+10 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186342113 "First, multiplying each component of $\\mathbf{c}$ by $-\\frac{1}{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{c} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the dot product, we add up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{c})\\cdot \\mathbf{d} & =0\\cdot 9+2\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =0+8+8-3-6 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186342114 "Starting from the equation $\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{w}=-2,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $x$ and then solve for $x$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{w} & =-2 \\\\ x\\cdot 3x+2x\\cdot (-3)+4\\cdot x+(-5)\\cdot 2 & =-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-6x+4x-10 & =-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2x-8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(3)(-8)}}{2(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{100}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 10}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm 5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get two solutions, ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=2.$ Since we need $x>0$, our final answer is $x=2$ only."] [17592186342115 "Starting from the equation $\\mathbf{l}\\cdot \\mathbf{m}=3,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $x$ and then solve for $x$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{l}\\cdot \\mathbf{m} & =3 \\\\ 5\\cdot 1+x\\cdot x+2\\cdot 4+x\\cdot (-1)+2x\\cdot (-3) & =3 \\\\ 5+{x}^{2}+8-x-6x & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-7x+13 & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-7x+10 & =0 \\\\ (x-5)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe get two solutions, ${x}_{1}=5$ and ${x}_{2}=2.$ Since we need $x>3$, our final answer is $x=5$ only."] [17592186344968 "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w}) & =\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{adding }\\mathbf{x}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x}))+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom }{\\color{blue}\\text{A2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{0}+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A4}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom  A3}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) & =\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{adding }-\\mathbf{w}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w}) & =\\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{w}+(-\\mathbf{w})) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom  A2}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{0} & =\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom }{\\color{blue}\\text{A4}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{0} & =\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom  A3}\\hspace{0.222em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186344987 "If $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $A\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\mathbf{b} & =\\mathbf{0} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2k \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4k-8 \\\\ 2k-4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4k-8 &=0 \\\\ 2k-4 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=2.$"] [17592186344988 "We need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -10 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is ${x}_{1}$, and we have two free variables, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ in terms of ${x}_{2}$  and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nFrom the first equation we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3},$ so the set of all the solutions of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in \\mathbb{R}\\right\\}.\n$$"] [17592186344989 "We need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & -9 \\\\ 6 & -18 & 18 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -18 & 18 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$\n\nThe second equation tells us that ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(0)+3{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-3{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the set of all the solutions of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}{x}_{3}\\in \\mathbb{R}\\right\\}.\n$$"] [17592186345025 "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(W),$ we reduce the matrix $W$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 9 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 