}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{w}}_{2}}=\\underbrace{(-3)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(0)}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{w}}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=(-3)\\cdot 0=0.$"] [17592186345149 "First, we reduce the matrix $H$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 12 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 9 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 9 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(H)$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{h}}_{1},{\\mathbf{h}}_{2},{\\mathbf{h}}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{h}}_{1},{\\mathbf{h}}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(H).$"] [17592186345151 "First, we reduce the matrix $V$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{10}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$"] [17592186351223 "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{\\mathbf{u}}_{1},{\\mathbf{u}}_{2},{\\mathbf{u}}_{3}\\},$ \n$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{\\mathbf{w}}_{1},{\\mathbf{w}}_{2}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\hspace{1em}\\text{and}\\hspace{1em}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{3}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{3} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2}=0\\hspace{1em}\\text{and}\\hspace{1em}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {\\mathbf{w}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\hspace{1em}\\text{and}\\hspace{1em}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {\\mathbf{w}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {\\mathbf{w}}_{3}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{3} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{u}}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=1+25+4=30\\ne 1 & {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{6}{13}+\\frac{6}{13}=0 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ {\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ {\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & {\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $V$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{18}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}=0 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ {\\mathbf{w}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{4}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}=1 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ {\\mathbf{w}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{w}}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} 